---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { u, getAnchors, anchorSlug } from '../lib/data.mjs';

const anchors = getAnchors();
const settled = anchors.filter((a) => a.accuracy);
---

<Layout title="Scoring Methodology v0.2">
  <div style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
    <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
      <h1 style="font-size: 2.25rem; font-weight: 800;">Scoring Methodology</h1>
      <span class="badge" style="background-color: #dbeafe; color: #1e40af; font-size: 0.85rem;">Version 0.2</span>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text-muted); max-width: 760px;">
      How Modafied evaluates data feeds across six objective dimensions and computes anchored accuracy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Core Principles</h2>
    <ul style="margin-left: 1.5rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.75rem; color: var(--text-color);">
      <li><strong>Mechanical computation:</strong> Every score is calculated automatically by open code running against immutable snapshot history. No manual score adjustments exist.</li>
      <li><strong>No sponsored placement:</strong> Feed evaluations cannot be purchased, sponsored, or altered by commercial agreements.</li>
      <li><strong>Rule H1 compliance:</strong> Accuracy is never published without an authoritative, independent settlement anchor named on the same card. In the absence of an anchor, accuracy is omitted.</li>
      <li><strong>Full reproducibility:</strong> Given the snapshot archive and the method version stamp, re-running the scoring code produces a byte-identical scorecard. The archive and the artifacts are public; the scoring code is not published yet, so this is not yet something an outside party can check for themselves. That gap, and what closes it, is on the <a href={u('/replicas')}>replication page</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>No revenue from anything graded:</strong> Nothing on a card is for sale, at any price, and the funding arrangement that makes that credible is written out on the <a href={u('/independence')}>independence page</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">The Six Dimensions</h2>

    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem;">
      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700;">1. Freshness Lag</h3>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.25rem;">
          Computed as the elapsed time between the capture time recorded by Modafied's clock (<code>captured_at</code>) and the event timestamp declared by the source itself (<code>source_timestamp</code>).
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          Reported as p50 (median) and p95 (tail risk) percentiles in seconds. This reveals real-world ingest latency and distinguishes primary publishers from lagging derivatives.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700;">2. Revision Behavior</h3>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.25rem;">
          Measures the proportion of historically published values that undergo retroactive modification for previously published event timestamps.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          Calculated by diffing consecutive snapshots and identifying field value changes where the reference period or event identifier has not moved forward.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700;">3. Correction Latency</h3>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.25rem;">
          Measures the time elapsed from an initial incorrect or provisional figure until the source publishes an updated, revised value.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          Reported as p50 and p95 in seconds across all detected revisions in the observation window.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700;">4. Completeness by Segment</h3>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.25rem;">
          Tracks the ratio of expected data fields successfully populated with valid, non-null data across discrete categories or geographic stations.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          Prevents aggregate masking where a feed appears reliable globally while dropping essential fields in specific regional endpoints.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700;">5. Schema Stability</h3>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.25rem;">
          Evaluates structural reliability by detecting key additions, deletions, or data type changes across consecutive snapshots.
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          Reported as the percentage of snapshots exhibiting zero schema modifications, highlighting feeds that pose operational breaking change risks.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary-color); padding-left: 1.25rem;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700;">6. Internal Consistency</h3>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.25rem;">
          Applies domain-specific sanity checks within individual response payloads (such as valid geographical bounding boxes, positive humidity limits, or totals equaling child component sums).
        </p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          Measures the proportion of snapshots that pass all structural boundary checks without internal contradictions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Anchored Accuracy (Rule H1)</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      Accuracy cannot be claimed in an epistemological vacuum. Modafied strictly adheres to Rule H1:
    </p>
    <blockquote style="border-left: 3px solid #2563eb; background: var(--bg-subtle); padding: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-style: italic;">
      "Never publish an accuracy number without naming the anchor on the same page. Lineage and disagreement are always allowed; truth is never claimed."
    </blockquote>
    <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      Today exactly one anchor is in force. National Weather Service forecasts are paired with the National Weather
      Service's own later observations for the same station and valid hour, admitted only when the observation was
      captured at or after the forecast. That is a <em>later observation of the same source</em>: it measures whether
      a publisher agrees with itself, not whether it agrees with the world, and it is labelled that way on every card
      that carries it. Anchors against an independent publisher of the same quantity are stronger, and none is in
      force yet.
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
      {settled.length === 0
        ? 'No anchor has settled any pairs yet, so no accuracy figure is published anywhere on this site.'
        : `Across the catalog, ${anchors.length} source${anchors.length === 1 ? '' : 's'} declare an anchor and ${settled.length} ${settled.length === 1 ? 'has' : 'have'} settled pairs. Samples are still small enough that the figures move; each is printed with its sample count so a reader can see how little is behind it.`}
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 0.95rem;">
      Every anchor, its settle rule, its provenance and how independent it actually is, is listed on the
      <a href={u('/anchors')}>anchor registry</a>, and every accuracy figure on this site links back to its entry
      there. Accuracy never enters the letter grade, because a grade that folds in accuracy quietly punishes every
      feed that has no anchor available.
      {settled.length > 0 && (
        <> Current entry: <a href={u(`/anchors#${anchorSlug(settled[0].source_id, settled[0].kind)}`)}>{settled[0].source_name}</a>.</>
      )}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Why the six dimensions constrain each other</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 820px;">
      Anything measured and published will eventually be optimised against, and a benchmark that can be gamed by
      changing one behaviour is worse than no benchmark, because it rewards the change. The six dimensions are
      chosen so that winning one by manipulation loses another. This is published rather than kept quiet: the trap
      only works if it is known.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width: 20rem;">Optimise this</th><th>And this gets worse</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Publish earlier to win <strong>freshness lag</strong></td>
          <td>More of what you publish turns out to need changing, which raises <strong>revision behaviour</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Stop issuing corrections to win <strong>revision behaviour</strong></td>
          <td>Wrong values stay in the feed and contradict its own later values, which shows up in <strong>internal consistency</strong> and lengthens <strong>correction latency</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Pad the payload with fields to win <strong>completeness</strong></td>
          <td>The shape of the response keeps moving, which costs <strong>schema stability</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Freeze the schema to win <strong>schema stability</strong></td>
          <td>New segments cannot be added where they belong, so coverage gaps surface as <strong>completeness</strong> by segment.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.92rem; max-width: 820px;">
      The honest way to win is the boring one: publish when the value is ready, correct it visibly when it changes,
      and keep the shape stable. There is no arrangement of the six that rewards anything else, and if one is found,
      that is a method bug and a <a href={u('/disputes')}>dispute</a> worth filing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 1rem;">What gets a scorecard</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 820px;">
      Data sources today. The method is written so that other things an agent depends on can be graded by the same
      rules, and each is listed here only when it is actually measured, not when it is planned.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width: 12rem;">Subject</th><th>What is measured</th><th style="width: 9rem;">State</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Sources</strong></td>
          <td>The six dimensions, plus accuracy where an anchor exists.</td>
          <td><span class="badge">Published</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>MCP servers</strong></td>
          <td>Tool count, schema bytes, whether provenance is returned, how untrusted text is handled, injection surface, latency.</td>
          <td><span class="badge">Not yet</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Nodes and paths</strong></td>
          <td>Accuracy against a named anchor, calibration error, cost per call, p95 latency, drift since the last grade; and for a path, its end-to-end accuracy against the product of its hops.</td>
          <td><span class="badge">Not yet</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.92rem; max-width: 820px;">
      The same constraint governs all three: no anchor, no accuracy. A subject in a class nothing can settle can
      still be measured on the properties its own behaviour reveals, and gets no accuracy number at all.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Revisiting and Method Bumps</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.95rem;">
      When scoring formulas or normalization steps are refined, the method version is incremented (e.g., from v0.1 to v0.2), and historical scorecards retain their original method stamp for permanent auditability.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
